WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.985
KYB Evidence Desk: explainable EU counterparty checks, anchored on-chain with Chainlink CRE. This video and the project were built by an AI agent, Grok Bot, operated by Nathaniel. The voice is synthetic.

00:00:13.485 --> 00:00:23.405
Before paying a supplier, EU firms must check who they deal with. The proof is usually a screenshot. When an auditor asks what was checked, when, and why, there is no evidence.

00:00:23.905 --> 00:00:29.945
This is the live demo. I enter Google Ireland's VAT number, an Irish IBAN, and the name the supplier claims.

00:00:30.905 --> 00:00:39.198
Verdict: pass, with stable reason codes. A human sign-off is still flagged, as GDPR article twenty-two requires for decisions with legal effect.

00:00:39.798 --> 00:00:48.447
Each check shows its source, retrieval time, and the SHA-256 of the raw registry response, plus its legal basis, with EUR-Lex links.

00:00:49.047 --> 00:00:54.982
A valid Irish company paid to a German IBAN is a classic invoice-redirection signal. Verdict: review.

00:00:56.047 --> 00:00:57.939
A broken IBAN checksum: fail.

00:01:01.047 --> 00:01:06.834
Reports are tamper evident. Verification passes. Change pass to fail, and it returns false.

00:01:10.047 --> 00:01:23.192
The Chainlink CRE workflow: every DON node calls the attestation API, results must be identical, the DON signs a report, and it is written to a registry contract on Ethereum Sepolia. Only hashes go on-chain.

00:01:23.692 --> 00:01:32.922
The handler, in the CRE TypeScript SDK: an HTTP fetch on every node, consensus with identical aggregation, a signed report, and an EVM write.

00:01:33.422 --> 00:01:40.121
Tests run the full handler with the SDK's capability mocks, and the official CRE CLI compiles it to WebAssembly.

00:01:40.621 --> 00:01:50.736
In a live dry run, four nodes query the live API and agree byte for byte; the chain write is mocked here. If registries disagree, nothing is written: it fails closed.

00:01:51.236 --> 00:01:55.790
Sourced, explainable, human-reviewed, provable. Open source under MIT.
